@page "/projects/fulcrum"
@layout ParkerPortfolio.Layouts.ProjectLayout

<PageTitle>Fulcrum · Aaron Joel Parker</PageTitle>

<div class="canvas">
    <a class="back" href="/">&larr; Portfolio</a>

    <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">The @P.Archetype</p>
        <h1 class="title">@P.Name</h1>
        <p class="tagline">@P.Tagline</p>
        <p class="status">@P.Status</p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
            Markets shout. Headlines compete for certainty even when the underlying
            filing barely justifies a shrug. Traders who want context, not noise,
            spend their day filtering — and miss the small signals that actually
            move a position.
        </p>

        <h2>The approach</h2>
        <p>
            Fulcrum ingests news and filings, asks an Azure OpenAI model for a careful
            read, and stores the result as time-series in TimescaleDB. The UI surfaces
            signal as evidence, never as a verdict. It is the information broker —
            calm, sourced, and unwilling to claim more than it knows. Not the bookie.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
            Clean Architecture in ASP.NET Core with Blazor Server on the read side,
            SignalR for live updates, EF Core over Timescale hypertables for the event
            log, and Azure OpenAI behind a careful prompt boundary.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <p class="stack-label">Stack</p>
        <div class="chips">
            @foreach (var tech in P.Stack)
            {
                <span class="chip">@tech</span>
            }
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly Project P = ProjectsCatalog.All.First(p => p.Slug == "fulcrum");
}
